    val = vsk->buffer_max_size;
if (val < vsk->buffer_min_size)
    val = vsk->buffer_min_size;
Reason:
The original order clamps to the maximum first, then to the minimum. If the user sets buffer_min_size larger than buffer_max_size, the minimum check overrides the maximum check, allowing vsk->buffer_size to exceed buffer_max_size, violating the intended memory boundaries.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (val < vsk->buffer_min_size)
    val = vsk->buffer_min_size;
if (val > vsk->buffer_max_size)
    val = vsk->buffer_max_size;
Reason:
The fix clamps to the minimum first, then to the maximum. This ensures that even if buffer_min_size exceeds buffer_max_size, the final value is capped at buffer_max_size, preventing vsk->buffer_size from exceeding the configured maximum.

